<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="sec-one-sided-limits">
  <title>One-sided Limits</title>

<insight xml:id="insight-one-sided-limits">
    <p>
        The phrase <q>from either side</q> used in the preceding definition is very important. 
        The notation we use <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to a^+} f(x)</m> to indicate the limit from the right and 
        <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to a^-} f(x)</m> to indicate the limit from the left. 
        If <term>both limits exist and are the same</term>, then we say the limit exists.
    </p>
</insight>

<statement>
    <p>
      Let <m>f(x) = x - 5</m>. First let's look at the meaning of <q>the limit of <m>f(x)</m> as <m>x</m> approaches 5 from the right.</q> This is written as:
      <me>\lim_{x \to 5^+} f(x) = \lim_{x \to 5^+} (x - 5)</me>
      In <m>x \to 5^+</m>, the <m>+</m> means we are approaching 5 from the positive side. This side is to the right of 5 on a number line. Now as <m>x</m> approaches 5 from the right, note that <m>x - 5</m> is always positive. The limit from the right is of course zero, but the fact that <m>x - 5</m> is always positive will be used later on.
    </p>
    <p>
      Now, <q>the limit of <m>f(x)</m> as <m>x</m> approaches 5 from the left</q> is the same notion. It is written as:
      <me>\lim_{x \to 5^-} f(x) = \lim_{x \to 5^-} (x - 5)</me>
      The <m>-</m> sign means we are approaching 5 from the negative side. This side is to the left of 5 on a number line. Note that as <m>x</m> approaches 5 from the left, <m>x - 5</m> is always negative. The limit from the left is of course zero, but we will make use of the fact that <m>x - 5</m> is always negative later on.
    </p>
  </statement>

<theorem xml:id="theorem-two-sided-limit">
  <title>Two-sided Limit</title>
  <statement>
    <p>
      A function <m>f</m> has the <term>limit</term> <m>L</m> as <m>x</m> approaches <m>a</m> 
      if the limit from the left exists and the limit from the right exists and both 
      limits are <m>L</m>. That is:
      <me>
        \lim_{x \to a^+} f(x) = \lim_{x \to a^-} f(x) = L \iff \lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L
      </me>
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>

<example xml:id="example-limit-one-over-x">
  <statement>
    <p>
      Show that <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{1}{x}</m> does not exist.
    </p>
  </statement>
  <solution>
    <p>
      To show that the two-sided limit does not exist, we evaluate the one-sided limits:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            As <m>x</m> approaches <m>0</m> from the right (<m>x \to 0^+</m>), the values of <m>x</m> are positive and decreasing, so <m>\frac{1}{x}</m> increases without bound:
            <me>\lim_{x \to 0^+} \frac{1}{x} = \infty</me>
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            As <m>x</m> approaches <m>0</m> from the left (<m>x \to 0^-</m>), the values of <m>x</m> are negative and increasing toward <m>0</m>, so <m>\frac{1}{x}</m> decreases without bound:
            <me>\lim_{x \to 0^-} \frac{1}{x} = -\infty</me>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
    <p>
      Since the one-sided limits are not equal (and do not approach a finite real number), the two-sided limit <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{1}{x}</m> <strong>does not exist</strong>.
    </p>
  </solution>
</example>



  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>H</m> defined as follows:
        <me>
          H(x)= \begin{cases} 2x+2 \amp \text{for } x \lt 1 \\ 2x-2 \amp \text{for } x \ge 1 \end{cases}
        </me>
        Graph the function and find each of the following limits, if they exist:
        <ol cols="2">
          <li><p><m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \rightarrow 1} H(x)</m></p></li>
          <li><p><m>\displaystyle\lim_{x \rightarrow -3} H(x)</m></p></li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
    <figure xml:id="fig-piecewise-h">
    <caption>Graph of <m>H(x)</m></caption>
    <image width="60%">
        <shortdescription>A piecewise function with a line y=2x+2 for x less than 1 and y=2x-2 for x greater than or equal to 1.</shortdescription>
        <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
                \begin{axis}[
                    axis lines = middle,
                    grid = both,
                    xlabel = \(x\),
                    ylabel = \(y\),
                    xmin=-2, xmax=4,
                    ymin=-2, ymax=6,
                    xtick={-2,-1,0,1,2,3,4},
                    ytick={-2,0,2,4,6},
                ]
                <!-- Piece 1: 2x + 2 for x < 1 -->
                \addplot [domain=-2:0.95, blue, thick] {2*x + 2};
                <!-- Open circle at (1, 4) -->
                \draw[blue, thick] (axis cs:1,4) circle [radius=2pt];

                <!-- Piece 2: 2x - 2 for x >= 1 -->
                \addplot [domain=1:3.5, blue, thick] {2*x - 2};
                <!-- Solid dot at (1, 0) -->
                \addplot[only marks, mark=*, blue] coordinates {(1,0)};
                \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
    </image>
</figure>

      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              Check one-sided limits at <m>x=1</m>:
              <me>\lim_{x \to 1^-} H(x) = 2(1)+2 = 4</me>
              <me>\lim_{x \to 1^+} H(x) = 2(1)-2 = 0</me>
              Since <m>4 \neq 0</m>, the limit <strong>does not exist (DNE)</strong>.
            </p>
          </li>
          <li>
            <p>
              At <m>x = -3</m>, we only use the first piece (<m>x \lt 1</m>):
              <me>\lim_{x \to -3} (2x+2) = 2(-3)+2 = -4</me>
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Show that <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to 0} \frac{\sin 2x}{5x}=\frac{2}{5}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        We use the special limit <m>\lim_{\theta \to 0} \frac{\sin \theta}{\theta} = 1</m>.
        Multiply the numerator and denominator to match the argument <m>2x</m>:
        <me>
          \lim_{x\to 0} \frac{\sin 2x}{5x} = \frac{1}{5} \lim_{x\to 0} \frac{\sin 2x}{x} \cdot \frac{2}{2}
        </me>
        <me>
          = \frac{2}{5} \lim_{x\to 0} \frac{\sin 2x}{2x} = \frac{2}{5}(1) = \frac{2}{5}
        </me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <statement>
      <p>Find <m>\displaystyle\lim_{x\to \infty} x\sin\frac{1}{x}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Let <m>\theta = 1/x</m>. As <m>x \to \infty</m>, <m>\theta \to 0^+</m>.
        <me>\lim_{x \to \infty} x \sin(1/x) = \lim_{\theta \to 0^+} \frac{1}{\theta} \sin \theta = \lim_{\theta \to 0^+} \frac{\sin \theta}{\theta} = 1</me>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>
</section>
